TASK A4 – DESIGN THE EXCEPTION STRATEGY (4 Marks)
A4.1 – Identify the Errors (2 Marks)
The following errors can occur in the hospital billing system:
1. Negative charges – Bill amount is less than zero.
2. Claim above cover – Insurance claim exceeds the policy cover limit.
3. Unknown package – Invalid government scheme package code.
4. Missing patient record – Patient details are not found.
5. Memory allocation failure – Memory allocation fails during program execution.
Exception Hierarchy
std::exception
      |
      +-- BillingError
      |       |
      |       +-- NegativeChargeError
      |       +-- MissingPatientError
      |       +-- UnknownPackageError
      |
      +-- ClaimError
      |       |
      |       +-- ClaimAboveCoverError
      |
      +-- std::bad_alloc


Custom exception classes are derived from std::exception so that errors can be handled using a common base class.
A4.2 – Specify Exceptions (2 Marks)
Functions that may throw:
- total() – may throw if invalid charges are detected.
- amountCovered() – may throw if the claim exceeds the cover limit.
- findPatient() – may throw if the patient record is missing.
- getPackageRate() – may throw if an unknown package is given.
- Memory allocation – may throw std::bad_alloc.
Functions that should not throw:
- Destructors should be marked noexcept.
- Cleanup functions should not throw exceptions.
Example:
virtual ~Bill() noexcept = default;


Destructors should not throw because an exception during stack unwinding can cause program termination.

Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
Instead of exceptions, errors can be reported using return codes such as enum Status or bool.

Example:
enum Status {
    SUCCESS,
    NEGATIVE_CHARGE,
    CLAIM_ERROR,
    UNKNOWN_PACKAGE
};


A function returns a status code to indicate whether the operation was successful.
Feature	Exceptions	Return Codes
Normal path time	O(1)	O(1)
Error path	Exception handling overhead	O(1)
Extra space	Exception object during error	Status/message storage
Code	Cleaner normal flow	More checks required
Error handling	Centralized using catch	Must check every return value

Conclusion: Exceptions are suitable for the billing system because they separate normal processing from error handling and allow errors to be handled at different levels of the program.
